#X text 33 19 The "panner" component of this instrument consists of another euclidean sequencer that advances on every HIT from the base pattern \, as opposed to advance on every clock tick. The goal here is to leverage the Euclidean Algorithm to determine if a hit should be panned to the left or to the right (depending on the values of "spread" and "hit side"). Since this sequencer only operates on HITS from the base pattern \, the length of its euclidean sequence is always the same as the number of HITS set for the base pattern.;
#X text 33 168 bang: advance the sequencer;
#X text 65 222 float: base pattern hits (scaled from 0.0-1.0 to 0.0-BASE_PATTERN_LENGTH), f 25;
#X text 255 237 float: panner hits knob value (0.0-1.0), f 18;
#X text 386 237 float: spread (0.0-1.0), f 18;
#X text 568 237 float: side to pan to on hit (0=L \, 1=R), f 18;
#X text 89 698 <--- apply the spread. NOTE: if spread = 0: this is the same as bypassing the 'panner' component. all base pattern hits will be centered regardless of pan_hits and pan_side, f 42;
#X text 422 352 if pan side is L \, we pan to the L on every hit of this sequencer and to the R on every rest. if the pan side is R \, we do the opposite -->, f 20;
#X text 33 819 float: pan value (-1.0-1.0). -1 = 100% L \, 0 = centered \, 1 = 100% R. any value in between distributes the audio signal based on the spread amount;
