#X text 92 28 The "dynamics" component of this instrument acts as a "velocity value generator" and consists of 2 parts: \; jitter: randomize velocity values under a certain range determined by the jitter_level and jitter_ceiling. \; \; accent generator: this is another euclidean sequencer that advances on every HIT from the base pattern \, as opposed to advance on every clock tick. The goal here is to leverage the Euclidean Algorithm to determine if a hit should be accented or not. When this sequencer emits a HIT \, we boost the velocity value generated by the "jitter generator" based on accent_intensity value. Since this sequencer only operates on HITS from the base pattern \, the length of its euclidean sequence is always the same as the number of HITS set for the base pattern. \;;
#X text 383 301 float: accent hits knob value (0.0-1.0), f 18;
#X text 521 301 float: accent shift knob value (0.0-1.0), f 18;
#X text 690 663 ^^^ accent headroom: how much can we add to velocities generated by the jitter component based on accent_intensity and jitter_ceiling., f 18;
#X text 341 732 jitter: generate random velocities in the 0.0-1.0 range, f 19;
#X text 185 587 ^^^ accent generator: boost the velocity values generated by the 'jitter' component on every HIT of this sequencer, f 26;
#X text 521 818 <-- scale the random jitter value taking into account jitter_ceiling and jitter_level: \; \; velocity = \$0-jitter-ceiling x (1 - random(0 \, 1) * $jitter-level, f 70;
#X text 296 864 <--- after emitting an accent \, reset the stored 'boost value' back to 0, f 30;
#X text 296 963 <--- add the accent 'boost value' (0 if the current hit is not accented);
#X text 947 316 float (0.0 - 1.0), f 18;
#X text 826 316 float: (0.0 - 1.0), f 15;
#X text 665 316 float: (0.1 - 1.0);
#X text 34 1053 float: generated velocity value (0.0-1.0), f 20;
#X text 286 1053 bang: accent generator HIT;
